    "title": "Concept 1: Virus Structure and Reproduction",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">Viruses are <strong>non-living</strong> particles composed of genetic material (either DNA or RNA) enclosed in a protein coat called a <em>capsid</em>. They cannot reproduce on their own and must invade a <strong>living host cell</strong>. Inside the host cell, viruses hijack the cell's machinery to produce many new viruses. This replication process often damages or destroys the host cell, causing tissue damage.</p>",
